WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.262
This example creates stay days in the CTE.

00:00:03.262 --> 00:00:07.432
Each CTE row represents one completed admission.

00:00:07.432 --> 00:00:13.597
The final query joins patients and calculates admission count and average stay per patient.

00:00:13.597 --> 00:00:17.052
The grain changes only in the final GROUP BY.

00:00:17.052 --> 00:00:21.167
That separation makes the transformation easier to validate.

00:00:21.167 --> 00:00:27.902
A CTE gives a name to an intermediate result so a complex query can be read in stages.

00:00:27.902 --> 00:00:35.807
Use it to clarify grain changes and major transformations, not simply to break every two lines into a

00:00:35.807 --> 00:00:43.970
new block. A useful CTE name describes what the rows represent, such as patient_totals or ranked_visits.
